So far we’ve seen <a href="https://pytgcalls.github.io/PyTgCalls/Decorators?ref=dev">how to register a decorator</a> function that executes every time an update comes from the server, but there’s much more than that to come. Here we’ll discuss <a href="https://pytgcalls.github.io/PyTgCalls/Filters?ref=dev">filters</a>. Filters enable a fine-grain control over what kind of updates are allowed or not to be passed in your callback functions, based on their inner details.<hr/><h2>Single Filters</h2>Let’s start right away with a simple example:<ul><li>This example will show you how to only handle updates that are an Incoming Call and ignore any other update. Filters are passed as the first argument of the decorator: <pre><code>from pytgcalls import filters
from pytgcalls.types import ChatUpdate
...
@app.on_update(filters.chat_update(ChatUpdate.Status.INCOMING_CALL))
def on_incoming_call(client, update):
    print(&#34;Incoming call!&#34;, update)</code></pre></li> <li>or, without decorators. Here filters are passed as the second argument of the add_handler function; the first is the callback function itself: <pre><code>from pytgcalls import filters
from pytgcalls.types import ChatUpdate
...
def on_incoming_call(client, update):
    print(&#34;Incoming call!&#34;, update)

pytgcalls.add_handler(on_incoming_call, filters.chat_update(ChatUpdate.Status.INCOMING_CALL))</code></pre></li></ul><h2>Combining Filters</h2>Filters can be used in a more advanced way by inverting and combining more filters together using bitwise operators <code>~</code>, <code>&amp;</code> and <code>|</code>: <ul><li>Use <code>~</code> to invert a filter (behaves like the <code>not</code> operator).</li> <li>Use <code>&amp;</code> and <code>|</code> to combine filters together (behaves like the <code>and</code> and <code>or</code> operators).</li></ul> Here are some examples: <ul><li>This example will show you how to only handle updates that are <b>left</b> updates from a specific <b>chat</b> and ignore any other update: <pre><code>from pytgcalls import filters
from pytgcalls.types import ChatUpdate
...
@app.on_update(~filters.chat_update(ChatUpdate.Status.LEFT_GROUP) &amp; filters.chat(1234567890))
def on_incoming_voice_call(client, update):
    print(&#34;Left chat!&#34;, update)</code></pre></li> <li>This example will show you how to only handle updates that are <b>left</b> updates or <b>incoming call</b> updates and from a specific <b>chat</b>: <pre><code>from pytgcalls import filters
from pytgcalls.types import ChatUpdate
...
@app.on_update(filters.chat(1234567890) &amp; (filters.chat_update(ChatUpdate.Status.LEFT_GROUP) | filters.chat_update(ChatUpdate.Status.INCOMING_CALL)))
def on_incoming_voice_call(client, update):
    print(&#34;Incoming voice call or left chat!&#34;, update)</code></pre></li></ul>

YouTube often blocks or rate-limits server IP addresses. Routing yt-dlp and FFmpeg through a proxy keeps the playback working when the IP of your server gets flagged.<hr/><h2>How it works</h2><ol><li><b>yt-dlp</b> resolves the direct media URLs, without downloading anything.</li> <li><b>FFmpeg</b> downloads and decodes the stream from those URLs.</li></ol>YouTube binds the resolved URLs to the IP address that requested them, so both steps must go through the same proxy.<hr/><h2>Setting up the proxy</h2><pre><code>PROXY = &#39;http://username:password@host:port&#39;
...
await app.play(
    chat_id,
    MediaStream(
        &#39;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=msiLgFkXvD8&#39;,
        ytdlp_parameters=f&#39;--proxy {PROXY}&#39;,
        ffmpeg_parameters=f&#39;-http_proxy {PROXY}&#39;,
    ),
)</code></pre><blockquote><b>IMPORTANT</b><br>FFmpeg only supports <b>HTTP</b> proxies. A SOCKS proxy passed only to yt-dlp is not enough, the download would come from a different IP address and fail.</blockquote><hr/><h2>Troubleshooting</h2>HTTP Error 403: Forbidden Only yt-dlp is using the proxy, pass it to <b>ffmpeg_parameters</b> too. HTTP Error 403 with a rotating proxy The exit IP changed between yt-dlp and FFmpeg, use a sticky session. Sign in to confirm you&#39;re not a bot The proxy IP address is flagged, switch to a residential proxy or pass your cookies with <b>--cookies cookies.txt</b>. Unable to connect to proxy Check the credentials and the format, it must be <b>http://username:password@host:port</b>.<hr/><h2>Using PinguProxy</h2><a href="https://pinguproxy.com/?ref=pytgcalls">PinguProxy</a> sponsors Py-TgCalls, the first 30 signups get 1GB for free. Two things matter when streaming:<ul><li><b>Prefer residential proxies</b>: YouTube flags datacenter IP ranges much more often, so they hit the bot check sooner.</li> <li><b>Always use a session</b>: rotating proxies change the exit IP on every connection, append <b>_session-&lt;id&gt;</b> to the username so yt-dlp and FFmpeg share the same IP address.</li></ul><pre><code>PROXY = (
    f&#39;http://username_session-{chat_id}:password&#39;
    &#39;@proxy.pinguproxy.com:12934&#39;
)</code></pre>Port <b>12934</b> routes through residential IPs and <b>12933</b> through datacenter ones. A session per chat keeps every stream on its own IP, add <b>_country-XX</b> before the session to pick the exit country.
